#* {{quote-book|author={{w|Robert Alfred Vaughan}}|title=Hours with the mystics : a contribution to the history of religious opinion|location=London|publisher=John W. Parker|year=1856|volume=1|oclc=793717967|page=45|pageurl=http://hdl.handle.net/2027/hvd.hw9y5h?urlappend=%3Bseq=67|passage=Thus, while the mystic of the [[theopathetic]] species is content to contemplate, to feel, or to act, suffering under Deity in his sublime passivity, the mysticism I term '''theosophic''' aspires to know and believes itself in possession of a certain supernatural divine faculty for that purpose.}}

